From 1a28e39f1a825297746244a3d19b56702ea40a05 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 15:01:07 +0500
Subject: [PATCH] Fix Postgres pool: enforce max_conn, queue with timeout, stop
 connection churn

Load-testing the pool (max_conn=8) against real Postgres found four problems
in document_store._cursor(), all fixed here:

1. max_conn was not enforced. SimpleConnectionPool is documented as not
   thread-safe and checks its cap before a connect finishes, so overlapping
   connects overshoot: 40 threads opened 28 server connections, 100 threads
   opened 39. FastAPI runs these sync routes on a threadpool.
   ThreadedConnectionPool enforces the cap but raises PoolError instantly
   when full (96 of 120 requests failed at 40 threads). Now: a
   ThreadedConnectionPool plus a BoundedSemaphore(max_conn), so callers queue
   up to PG_POOL_ACQUIRE_TIMEOUT_SECONDS (default 10) and then get
   UpstreamTimeoutError (504, retryable). Slots are released on every path.
2. min_conn=1 made every concurrent request open and discard a fresh Postgres
   connection (psycopg2 closes returned connections beyond min_conn idle):
   3,000 -> ~100 calls/s once concurrency passed min_conn. min_conn now
   defaults to max_conn (flat ~5,000 calls/s). max_conn is configurable via
   PG_POOL_MAX_CONN (default 8).
3. Persistent connections the server drops (idle timeout, failover, proxy
   restart) surfaced as OperationalError because psycopg2's conn.closed stays
   0. Checkout now discards dead idle connections via a zero-timeout select().
4. init_pool() raced on first use (check-then-set), able to create several
   pools. Now double-checked under a lock.

New metrics: auditflow_pg_pool_wait_seconds, auditflow_pg_pool_timeouts_total.

Adds tests/integration/test_pg_pool.py (7 tests, each verified to fail when
its fix is reverted) and tests/load/pg_pool_load.py (refuses to run unless the
DB name contains "test" or "load").

Load results, login DB work, 64 concurrent, 2 vCPU sandbox:
- 0 ms RTT: 1,200-1,660 logins/s at every pool size (was 40-50/s for pool>=4).
- 30 ms RTT: 31 / 63 / 125 / 244 logins/s at pool 2 / 4 / 8 / 16 (= pool /
  64 ms hold time), zero errors, peak server connections == pool size.
- bcrypt verify is ~320 ms CPU per login: ~6 logins/s on 2 cores, far below
  what max_conn=8 can serve, so the pool is not the login bottleneck.

